{"name":"Silicate Green","hex":"#88b2a9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silicate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any salt of silica or of one of the silicic acids; any mineral composed of silicates."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silicate"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silicate","blocks":[{"text":"A silicate is any member of a family of polyatomic anions consisting of silicon and oxygen, usually with the general formula [SiO(4−2x)−4−x]n, where 0 ≤ x < 2. The family includes orthosilicate SiO4−4, metasilicate SiO2−3, and pyrosilicate Si2O6−7. The name is also used for any salt of such anions, such as sodium metasilicate; or any ester containing the corresponding chemical group, such as tetramethyl orthosilicate. The name \"silicate\" is sometimes extended to any anions containing silicon, even if they do not fit the general formula or contain other atoms besides oxygen; such as hexafluorosilicate [SiF6]2−. Most commonly, silicates are encountered as silicate minerals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Orthosilicate-2D-dimensions.png/330px-Orthosilicate-2D-dimensions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicate","wikidata":"Q7130787"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Silicate-single-chain-plan-view-2D.png/500px-Silicate-single-chain-plan-view-2D.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Silicate-single-chain-plan-view-2D.png/500px-Silicate-single-chain-plan-view-2D.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of a metasilicate chain, emphasizing the tetrahedral silicate subunits.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilicate-single-chain-plan-view-2D.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Na2SiO3idealized.png/500px-Na2SiO3idealized.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Na2SiO3idealized.png/500px-Na2SiO3idealized.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alternative depiction of a metasilicate chain emphasizing the Si–O bonds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANa2SiO3idealized.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Silicate-double-chain-plan-view-2D.png/500px-Silicate-double-chain-plan-view-2D.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Silicate-double-chain-plan-view-2D.png/500px-Silicate-double-chain-plan-view-2D.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Double chain tetrahedra.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilicate-double-chain-plan-view-2D.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Silicate-sheet-3D-polyhedra.png/500px-Silicate-sheet-3D-polyhedra.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Silicate-sheet-3D-polyhedra.png/500px-Silicate-sheet-3D-polyhedra.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sheet silicates.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilicate-sheet-3D-polyhedra.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Coccolithus_pelagicus.png/330px-Coccolithus_pelagicus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Coccolithus_pelagicus.png/330px-Coccolithus_pelagicus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoccolithus_pelagicus.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_eme